            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

We had identified that your house has ALOT of excess air leakage heat loss associated with the following locations:



** There is significant air leakage coming from the lower foundation area. I highly suspect the foundation walls (front, left &amp; back) are compromised and allowing air leakage &amp; heat loss. Yes, there is a full vapour barrier down there, however it was puffed up like a big balloon during the blower door air leakage test. This must be why there are tires and pieces of lumber down there strategically placed to keep the vapour barrier down.



** The amount of air coming up through the foundation access panel was significant. Even if the access panel was closed, the air leakage would still occur as the heating ductwork travels through the foundation and there are openings to blow heat into the foundation to keep the floor below your feet warm. During the winter when it is cold &amp; windy outside, warm heated air is being sucked out of your foundation while replacing it with cold outside air - this is why you feel so cold on the main floor. I think you need to talk with your Condo Board about an opportunity to fix the foundations walls of your unit.



** The front &amp; back door are in need of new weather-stripping and an adjustment so they close tightly



** The windows are "slider" units that are not sealing well at all. All of the 2nd floor windows are in really bad shape and the front window on the main floor is broken.



** The 6" combustion air supply pipe and the chimney for the current furnace and hot water tank



Savings: The software models a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 119 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ. If you are able to resolve most of the air leakage locations outlined above, I suspect you will exceed this target by another 20%, resulting in more significant annual energy savings &amp; qualify for up to $1,300 in rebates. The major benefit for you is that the main floor will feel much more comfortable !!



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

There are 7 windows in your house that are original and many of them are in bad shape and allow a lot of air leakage heat loss. I modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing the 7 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 218 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.3 GJ, and qualify for either $1,137.50 or up to $ 2,275.00 (depends on the energy efficiency rating) in rebates through the Canada Greener Homes Program.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil hot water tank. In the last 10 years in Yellowknife there are less than 5 new houses constructed here that chose either oil or electricity as their fuel source for hot water. In fact, a very high percentage have moved towards high efficiency on-demand propane water heaters. As you have an end unit, it appears you should be able to vent this equipment out through the left side header space of your home. For your future planning, I have modeled an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.92 EF, could potentially save $ 79 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



One of the side benefits that is associated with upgrading your oil furnace &amp; hot water tank to high efficiency propane equipment is that you get to eliminate the chimney &amp; the 6" combustion air supply pipe, which are 2 big causes of air leakage heat loss for your home. The savings associated with this is included in the Air Sealing section of this report.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 85% efficient oil furnace, that is co-vented with the oil hot water tank up a chimney. In conjunction with the hot water upgrade recommendation, I have modeled 2 furnace upgrade options for you:



1. An ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

- This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



2. An ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

- This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,600 rebate from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, plus a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Both of these upgrades will eliminate the need for the current chimney and the 6" open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room. The direct venting for the new furnace should be able to be routed out through the header space on the left side of your house between the main &amp; second floor.



Savings:

1. By replacing your current propane furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 490 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



2. By replacing your current propane furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 532 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $3,100 in rebates from the combined GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP) &amp; the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities.



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an older gas or propane fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas or propane fired furnace. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 3 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front facing roof (East), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 2,324 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 767, and qualify for $3,900 from the Canada Greener Homes program.

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



NOTE: You have not yet applied to Greenerr Homes so the information in this report is for information purposes only and not applicable unless you apply for and are approved for participation in the Greener Homes Program by Natural Resources Canada (NRCan)



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the kitchen range hood fan, both bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and hot water tank are co-vented up the same chimney and could be suceptible to back-drafting combustion fumes into the house. However, the mechanical room does have a 6: combustion air supply pipe to help mitigate potential back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors.Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - 2 x bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer

        - The furnace has a fresh air duct connected to the return air plenum. This provides:

           - A bit of ourtside resh air each time the furnace is running

           - Make-up air each time an exhaust fan is operating or an upper floor window is open

           - The fresh air duct has a "damper" that you canj manually adjust for more/less fresh air



You may want to install a timer switch for the bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs Â Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.40 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



